WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.310
Now that they've gotten a good chance to see
him, what do fans think of the new Joker?

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:08.849
Are they comparing him to Heath Ledger's iconic
performance positively or negatively?

00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:11.700
Keep watching to find out the full scope of
opinion.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.079
Minor spoilers for "The Batman" are ahead.

00:00:14.079 --> 00:00:18.360
Barry Keoghan only appears as The Joker in
a single scene of "The Batman," where he bonds

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.500
with The Riddler in Arkham State Hospital.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.610
The brief glimpse audiences got of him left
them hungry for more, and that appetite was

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:29.080
sated with the release of a deleted scene
in which Batman visits Joker to ask for help

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.400
tracking down The Riddler.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.750
The five-minute scene gives us our best look
yet at Keoghan's Clown Prince of Crime, and

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.220
it's a sight to behold.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:40.051
Joker's face is blurred through the glass
barrier between him and the Dark Knight, but

00:00:40.051 --> 00:00:43.350
we get some close-ups on his nightmarish grin
and sunken eyes.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:47.571
Meanwhile, the back of his head is a gruesome
patchwork of green hair and what appear to

00:00:47.571 --> 00:00:48.680
be burn wounds.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.420
When Batman shows Joker a file on The Riddler,
Joker taunts him, telling him that The Riddler's

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.540
tactics are similar to his own.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.969
He suggests that Batman is having a hard time
catching the killer because, deep down…

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:03.780
“Cause you're not sure he's wrong, huh?

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.050
You think they deserved it, huh?"

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.800
Many fans wish the scene had been included
in the theatrical cut of the film.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.070
Keoghan definitely gets to show off his range,
especially when compared to his recent turn

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:18.310
as the conflicted hero Druig in Marvel's "Eternals."

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:23.100
Across social media, fans of "The Batman''
went wild over the scene mere moments after

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.580
it was released, and struck up vigorous discussion
of Keoghan's new take on the Joker.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.710
When Batman visits Joker, there's an immediate
implication that the two have already tangled.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:36.189
Pattinson's Batman has only been on his crime
fighting mission for just shy of two years,

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:39.010
but has already put his archnemesis behind
bars.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:40.010
Joker remarks,

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.570
"Almost our anniversary, isn't it?"

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:46.090
…so it seems they first crossed paths about
a year ago.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.700
He also seems to be intricately familiar with
how Batman thinks.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.600
Many fans loved the dynamic.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:52.850
One YouTube commenter wrote,

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:57.910
"This Clarice-Hannibal dynamic between Batman
and Joker has so much potential, I hope they

00:01:57.910 --> 00:01:59.100
do more of this."

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.610
Another user echoed the sentiment, writing,

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:05.560
"I love the fact that they have an already
established history and dynamic with one another."

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.940
On the DC_Cinematic subreddit, users seemed
to agree.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:09.940
One opined,

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:14.370
"Loving [Pattinson's] Batman, emotionally
a brick wall, still learning but overconfident

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.870
whilst still growing into the Batman persona.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:21.260
Batman doesn't appreciate the danger of going
to Joker, feeding into his mania and perception

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:24.840
of their relationship, really fascinating
acting work by both."

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.510
Keoghan's Joker is by far the most grotesque
we've seen on the big screen, a stark contrast

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:34.010
to the more realistic version Joaquin Phoenix
played in "Joker" and even outdoing Heath

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.950
Ledger's scarred appearance in "The Dark Knight."

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:40.780
Beneath layers of prosthetics and makeup,
the "Eternals" star is barely recognizable.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.430
Patchy hair gives way to hideous, blistered
wounds across his head and hands, and his

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.610
mangled grin is a lopsided maw of mismatched
teeth.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.140
It's a bold look, and fans seem to be split
on it.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:55.069
On the DC_Cinematic and Batman subreddits,
some fans were unsure what to think of Keoghan's

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:57.370
wounded appearance, with one commenting,

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:01.550
"Disfigured face is fine I think, and I know
he's supposed to have a congenital disorder

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:03.379
so disgusting mouth, I get it.

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:05.670
But I definitely don't like that he's almost
bald."

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:06.720
Another chimed in,

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.730
"The makeup on Barry's Joker is actually pretty
cool.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:10.910
Gives heavy Heath Ledger vibes.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:12.550
Not a fan of the lost hair though."

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:14.989
Other fans immediately loved what they were
seeing.

00:03:14.989 --> 00:03:15.989
One enthused,

00:03:15.989 --> 00:03:18.849
"THIS is what [I’ve] always wanted the Joker
to look like in live-action.

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:20.490
Creepy and monstrous as hell."

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.360
And a few hated it, of course.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.810
One fan wrote that the design was "terrible,"
adding,

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:28.170
"They do know it wasn't the make up that made
ledger a great joker.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:32.920
It's like someone said ohh the scars were
cool what if we gave him scars on his scars."

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:37.050
Even with the criticism, something about this
new Joker is resonating with fans.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:41.410
It's saying something that fans find Keoghan's
performance as worthy of comparison to Ledger's

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:44.159
iconic Joker in Christopher Nolan's "The Dark
Knight."

00:03:44.159 --> 00:03:47.940
When Keoghan posted a still from the scene
on his Twitter account, a fan replied,

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.920
"It's sacrilegious, but you might have just
aced Heath Ledger in a 4 minute clip.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:52.920
Bloody brilliant."

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.290
"If you're good at something, never do it
for free."

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.850
Not everyone was as pleased with the resemblance,
though.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:03:59.850
Another fan wrote,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:04.120
"Can we stop making every new cinematic iteration
of the joker a repeat of Heath Ledger?

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:07.780
Like yeah Heath nailed the role but that doesn't
mean you should repeatedly try to replicate

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.349
the style and energy he brought to the character."

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:14.430
Another fan countered that Keoghan isn't doing
a repeat of Ledger's performance; there are

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.839
just common elements to the Joker as a character:

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:20.920
"I don't get why people say Barry Keoghan
is doing a Heath Ledger impression in The

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:21.920
Batman.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.439
That's just the Joker."

